{"type":"message","id":"9b1790ef","parentId":"e3d9d69a","timestamp":"2026-03-26T11:02:10.541Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_lzwrt4hp0kej_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"author_association\":\"CONTRIBUTOR\",\"body\":\"\\u003ch3\\u003eGreptile Summary\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nThis PR primarily clarifies the `openclaw message` documentation (English and Chinese) to distinguish core built-in channel surfaces from plugin-provided channels, and adds a note that plugin channels may also expose `openclaw message` support. It also contains an unrelated change removing the `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` entry from the feishu plugin manifest and its generated metadata counterpart.\\\\n\\\\n**Key concerns:**\\\\n- **Unintentional skills regression (P1):** `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` is removed from `extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json` and the generated `bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts`, even though the `./skills` directory still contains four active skill definitions (`feishu-doc`, `feishu-drive`, `feishu-perm`, `feishu-wiki`). This silently de-registers all feishu skills — completely out of scope for a docs-only PR and almost certainly unintentional.\\\\n- **Generated i18n file edited directly (P2):** `docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md` is a generated file that should only be updated by running `scripts/docs-i18n`. The manual edit leaves the `source_hash` frontmatter stale, which will cause drift detection issues on future pipeline runs.\\\\n- The English `docs/cli/message.md` changes themselves are clear, accurate, and well-scoped.\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eConfidence Score: 2/5\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nNot safe to merge as-is — the feishu skills removal is likely an unintentional regression that would silently break feishu doc/drive/wiki/perm skills for all users.\\n\\nThe documentation changes are good, but the accidental removal of `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` from the feishu plugin is a P1 issue that de-registers four real, active skills. This regression needs explicit confirmation or revert before merging, and the zh-CN file should be regenerated through the pipeline rather than hand-edited.\\n\\n`extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json` and `src/plugins/bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts` — the skills removal must be explicitly confirmed or reverted. `docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md` should be regenerated via the i18n pipeline.\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003e\\u003ch3\\u003eImportant Files Changed\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n| Filename | Overview |\\n|----------|----------|\\n| docs/cli/message.md | Documentation clarified to distinguish core built-in channels from plugin-provided channels; new paragraph and bullet point added. Content is accurate and well-scoped. |\\n| docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md | Chinese translation manually updated to mirror English changes, but this file is generated and should not be directly edited; the `source_hash` frontmatter is now stale relative to the new English source. |\\n| extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json | Removes `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` from the feishu plugin manifest, but the `./skills` directory (feishu-doc, feishu-drive, feishu-perm, feishu-wiki) still exists — this appears to be an unintentional regression that de-registers all feishu skills. |\\n| src/plugins/bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts | Generated metadata file mirrors the skills removal from the feishu plugin JSON; carries the same regression risk as the source manifest change. |\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c!-- greptile_failed_comments --\\u003e\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003e\\u003ch3\\u003eComments Outside Diff (1)\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n1. `docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md`, line 1-14 ([link](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/2c203a8d73066faf76ca4fa5984e46caab0e9532/docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md#L1-L14)) \\n\\n   \\u003ca href=\\\"#\\\"\\u003e\\u003cimg alt=\\\"P2\\\" src=\\\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p2.svg?v=7\\\" align=\\\"top\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003e **Direct edit of generated i18n file; `source_hash` is now stale**\\n\\n   Per project guidelines (`CLAUDE.md`), `docs/zh-CN/**` is a generated file and should not be edited directly. The correct flow is: update English docs → run `scripts/docs-i18n` → apply targeted fixes only if instructed.\\n\\n   This file was edited by hand. As a result, the `source_hash` in the frontmatter (`35159baf...`) still reflects the old English source content before this PR's changes. Any future run of the i18n pipeline will see the Chinese file as out of sync with the English source and may overwrite these manually-applied translations.\\n\\n   Please regenerate this file via the i18n pipeline after the English changes are finalized.\\n\\n   \\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003ePrompt To Fix With AI\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n   `````markdown\\n   This is a comment left during a code review.\\n   Path: docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md\\n   Line: 1-14\\n\\n   Comment:\\n   **Direct edit of generated i18n file; `source_hash` is now stale**\\n\\n   Per project guidelines (`CLAUDE.md`), `docs/zh-CN/**` is a generated file and should not be edited directly. The correct flow is: update English docs → run `scripts/docs-i18n` → apply targeted fixes only if instructed.\\n\\n   This file was edited by hand. As a result, the `source_hash` in the frontmatter (`35159baf...`) still reflects the old English source content before this PR's changes. Any future run of the i18n pipeline will see the Chinese file as out of sync with the English source and may overwrite these manually-applied translations.\\n\\n   Please regenerate this file via the i18n pipeline after the English changes are finalized.\\n\\n   How can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n   `````\\n   \\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003c!-- /greptile_failed_comments --\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003ePrompt To Fix All With AI\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n`````markdown\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json\\nLine: 3\\n\\nComment:\\n**Skills entry removed while `./skills` directory still exists**\\n\\nThis PR removes `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` from the feishu plugin manifest, but the `./skills` directory is still fully populated with four skill definitions: `feishu-doc`, `feishu-drive`, `feishu-perm`, and `feishu-wiki`. Removing this entry means those skills will no longer be registered with the plugin, silently breaking feishu document/drive/wiki/permissions skills for any user with the feishu plugin installed.\\n\\nEvery other skills-carrying extension (`acpx`, `tavily`, `diffs`, `open-prose`) retains its `\\\"skills\\\"` entry — this removal appears accidental given the PR is scoped to documentation clarifications. The same line is also removed from `src/plugins/bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts` (line 839), compounding the impact.\\n\\nPlease confirm whether this removal is intentional. If not, both files need to be reverted.\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md\\nLine: 1-14\\n\\nComment:\\n**Direct edit of generated i18n file; `source_hash` is now stale**\\n\\nPer project guidelines (`CLAUDE.md`), `docs/zh-CN/**` is a generated file and should not be edited directly. The correct flow is: update English docs → run `scripts/docs-i18n` → apply targeted fixes only if instructed.\\n\\nThis file was edited by hand. As a result, the `source_hash` in the frontmatter (`35159baf...`) still reflects the old English source content before this PR's changes. Any future run of the i18n pipeline will see the Chinese file as out of sync with the English source and may overwrite these manually-applied translations.\\n\\nPlease regenerate this file via the i18n pipeline after the English changes are finalized.\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n`````\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003csub\\u003eReviews (1): Last reviewed commit: [\\\"docs(cli/message): clarify plugin extens...\\\"](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/2c203a8d73066faf76ca4fa5984e46caab0e9532) | [Re-trigger Greptile](https://app.greptile.com/api/retrigger?id=26413243)\\u003c/sub\\u003e\",\"created_at\":\"2026-03-26T07:44:00Z\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/55008#issuecomment-4132388510\",\"id\":4132388510,\"issue_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/55008\",\"node_id\":\"IC_kwDOQb6kR872Tz6e\",\"performed_via_github_app\":{\"client_id\":\"Iv1.5ad00a8cea2f6701\",\"created_at\":\"2024-04-02T00:16:23Z\",\"description\":\"Greptile is an AI agent that reviews pull requests with full context of your codebase. It can catch hidden bugs, security vulnerabilities, and anti-patterns. \",\"events\":[\"issues\",\"issue_comment\",\"pull_request\",\"pull_request_review\",\"pull_request_review_comment\",\"pull_request_review_thread\"],\"external_url\":\"https://greptile.com/\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/greptile-apps\",\"id\":867647,\"name\":\"Greptile Apps\",\"node_id\":\"A_kwHOCFqEf84ADT0_\",\"owner\":{\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/140149887?v=4\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/events{/privacy}\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/gists{/gist_id}\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/greptileai\",\"id\":140149887,\"login\":\"greptileai\",\"node_id\":\"O_kgDOCFqEfw\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/orgs\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/received_events\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/repos\",\"site_admin\":false,\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/subscriptions\",\"type\":\"Organization\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\"},\"permissions\":{\"actions\":\"read\",\"checks\":\"write\",\"contents\":\"write\",\"deployments\":\"read\",\"issues\":\"write\",\"metadata\":\"read\",\"pull_requests\":\"write\",\"secret_scanning_alerts\":\"read\",\"security_events\":\"read\",\"statuses\":\"write\",\"vulnerability_alerts\":\"read\"},\"slug\":\"greptile-apps\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-11T23:07:22Z\"},\"reactions\":{\"+1\":0,\"-1\":0,\"confused\":0,\"eyes\":0,\"heart\":0,\"hooray\":0,\"laugh\":0,\"rocket\":0,\"total_count\":0,\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/comments/4132388510/reactions\"},\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-26T07:44:09Z\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/comments/4132388510\",\"user\":{\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/867647?v=4\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/greptile-apps\",\"id\":165735046,\"login\":\"greptile-apps[bot]\",\"node_id\":\"BOT_kgDOCeDqhg\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/orgs\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/received_events\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/repos\",\"site_admin\":false,\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions\",\"type\":\"Bot\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\"}}\n{\"author_association\":\"CONTRIBUTOR\",\"body\":\"\\u003ch3\\u003eGreptile Summary\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nThis PR adds documentation to `docs/channels/feishu.md` and `docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md` to explain the Feishu routing fallback chain and session key format. The intent is worthwhile — closing a diagnostics gap noted in issue #54952 — but both changed files contain the same factual inaccuracy about the role of `groupPolicy`.\\n\\n**Key issues:**\\n\\n- **Inaccurate agent-selection claim (both files):** `channels.defaults.groupPolicy` is an access-control mechanism (`open` / `allowlist` / `disabled`) that determines whether a group message is accepted, not which agent handles it. The added text in `feishu.md` says it is applied \\\"to determine which agent handles it,\\\" and the `configuration-reference.md` addition says it is used \\\"to select the handling agent.\\\" Both phrases are incorrect and could mislead operators trying to debug wrong-agent routing. The actual agent selection in the fallback path is governed by `channels.feishu.defaultAccount` (already mentioned correctly in the same sentence in `feishu.md`).\\n\\n- The session-key and fallback-chain concepts themselves are a good addition, but the `groupPolicy` role needs to be corrected in both files before this lands.\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eConfidence Score: 3/5\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nNot safe to merge as-is — both files contain a factual inaccuracy about `groupPolicy` that would mislead operators debugging routing issues.\\n\\nThe fallback-chain and session-key additions are directionally correct and address a real documentation gap, but the same incorrect claim (that `groupPolicy` selects the handling agent) appears in both changed files. Since this is an operator-facing debugging aid, publishing inaccurate information about how agent selection works would actively harm the users this PR is trying to help. A targeted correction to the `groupPolicy` description in both files is needed before landing.\\n\\nBoth `docs/channels/feishu.md` (line 608) and `docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md` (line 86) need the `groupPolicy` agent-selection language corrected.\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003e\\u003ch3\\u003eImportant Files Changed\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n| Filename | Overview |\\n|----------|----------|\\n| docs/channels/feishu.md | Adds a fallback-chain paragraph under 'Multi-agent routing,' but incorrectly describes `channels.defaults.groupPolicy` as determining which agent handles the message rather than controlling access (open/allowlist/disabled). |\\n| docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md | Expands the `channels.defaults.groupPolicy` bullet to describe the fallback chain, but the added text inaccurately says `groupPolicy` is used 'to select the handling agent' when it is actually an access-control gate. |\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c!-- greptile_other_comments_section --\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003ePrompt To Fix All With AI\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n`````markdown\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: docs/channels/feishu.md\\nLine: 608\\n\\nComment:\\n**`groupPolicy` controls access, not agent selection**\\n\\nThe sentence states the gateway \\\"applies the **default group policy** (`channels.defaults.groupPolicy`) to determine which agent handles it,\\\" but this is inaccurate. `groupPolicy` is an access-control value (`open` / `allowlist` / `disabled`) that decides whether a group message is *accepted at all* — not which agent processes it. The handling agent in the fallback case is determined by the default account (already mentioned in the same sentence), not by `groupPolicy`.\\n\\nConsider rewording to separate these two concerns:\\n\\n```suggestion\\nWhen no explicit binding matches an incoming message, the gateway falls back to the **default account** (configured via `channels.feishu.defaultAccount` or the first configured account) to select the handling agent. The **default group policy** (`channels.defaults.groupPolicy`) then determines whether the message is accepted (`open`/`allowlist`/`disabled`). The resulting canonical session key (e.g., `feishu:g-{chatId}` for groups) reflects the final routing destination but does not encode which rule — binding or fallback — was responsible.\\n```\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md\\nLine: 86\\n\\nComment:\\n**`groupPolicy` selects access, not the handling agent**\\n\\nThe added sentence \\\"the gateway applies this default policy to **select the handling agent**\\\" misrepresents what `groupPolicy` does. As confirmed by the source implementation in `src/security/dm-policy-shared.ts` and `src/security/audit-channel.ts`, `groupPolicy` is an access-control gate (`open` / `allowlist` / `disabled`) — it determines whether a group message is allowed through, not which agent it is dispatched to. Agent selection in the fallback case is governed by `defaultAccount`, not `groupPolicy`.\\n\\n```suggestion\\n- `channels.defaults.groupPolicy`: fallback group policy when a provider-level `groupPolicy` is unset. Accepted values are `open`, `allowlist`, and `disabled`; this policy controls whether inbound group messages are accepted when no explicit binding matches, not which agent handles them. The session key's canonical form reflects the final destination but does not indicate whether routing matched a binding or the fallback chain.\\n```\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n`````\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003csub\\u003eReviews (1): Last reviewed commit: [\\\"docs: clarify Feishu routing fallback ch...\\\"](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/c6f8f3f44b4c67a83b8e378ef75363870d95e027) | [Re-trigger Greptile](https://app.greptile.com/api/retrigger?id=26413689)\\u003c/sub\\u003e\",\"created_at\":\"2026-03-26T07:50:24Z\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/55013#issuecomment-4132433687\",\"id\":4132433687,\"issue_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/55013\",\"node_id\":\"IC_kwDOQb6kR872T-8X\",\"performed_via_github_app\":{\"client_id\":\"Iv1.5ad00a8cea2f6701\",\"created_at\":\"2024-04-02T00:16:23Z\",\"description\":\"Greptile is an AI agent that reviews pull requests with full context of your codebase. It can catch hidden bugs, security vulnerabilities, and anti-patterns. \",\"events\":[\"issues\",\"issue_comment\",\"pull_request\",\"pull_request_review\",\"pull_request_review_comment\",\"pull_request_review_thread\"],\"external_url\":\"https://greptile.com/\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/greptile-apps\",\"id\":867647,\"name\":\"Greptile Apps\",\"node_id\":\"A_kwHOCFqEf84ADT0_\",\"owner\":{\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/140149887?v=4\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/events{/privacy}\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/gists{/gist_id}\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/greptileai\",\"id\":140149887,\"login\":\"greptileai\",\"node_id\":\"O_kgDOCFqEfw\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/orgs\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/received_events\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/repos\",\"site_admin\":false,\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/subscriptions\",\"type\":\"Organization\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\"},\"permissions\":{\"actions\":\"read\",\"checks\":\"write\",\"contents\":\"write\",\"deployments\":\"read\",\"issues\":\"write\",\"metadata\":\"read\",\"pull_requests\":\"write\",\"secret_scanning_alerts\":\"read\",\"security_events\":\"read\",\"statuses\":\"write\",\"vulnerability_alerts\":\"read\"},\"slug\":\"greptile-apps\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-11T23:07:22Z\"},\"reactions\":{\"+1\":0,\"-1\":0,\"confused\":0,\"eyes\":0,\"heart\":0,\"hooray\":0,\"laugh\":0,\"rocket\":0,\"total_count\":0,\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/comments/4132433687/reactions\"},\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-26T07:50:24Z\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/comments/4132433687\",\"user\":{\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/867647?v=4\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/greptile-apps\",\"id\":165735046,\"login\":\"greptile-apps[bot]\",\"node_id\":\"BOT_kgDOCeDqhg\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/orgs\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/received_events\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/repos\",\"site_admin\":false,\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions\",\"type\":\"Bot\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\"}}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":1864,"aggregated":"{\"author_association\":\"CONTRIBUTOR\",\"body\":\"\\u003ch3\\u003eGreptile Summary\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nThis PR primarily clarifies the `openclaw message` documentation (English and Chinese) to distinguish core built-in channel surfaces from plugin-provided channels, and adds a note that plugin channels may also expose `openclaw message` support. It also contains an unrelated change removing the `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` entry from the feishu plugin manifest and its generated metadata counterpart.\\\\n\\\\n**Key concerns:**\\\\n- **Unintentional skills regression (P1):** `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` is removed from `extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json` and the generated `bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts`, even though the `./skills` directory still contains four active skill definitions (`feishu-doc`, `feishu-drive`, `feishu-perm`, `feishu-wiki`). This silently de-registers all feishu skills — completely out of scope for a docs-only PR and almost certainly unintentional.\\\\n- **Generated i18n file edited directly (P2):** `docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md` is a generated file that should only be updated by running `scripts/docs-i18n`. The manual edit leaves the `source_hash` frontmatter stale, which will cause drift detection issues on future pipeline runs.\\\\n- The English `docs/cli/message.md` changes themselves are clear, accurate, and well-scoped.\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eConfidence Score: 2/5\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nNot safe to merge as-is — the feishu skills removal is likely an unintentional regression that would silently break feishu doc/drive/wiki/perm skills for all users.\\n\\nThe documentation changes are good, but the accidental removal of `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` from the feishu plugin is a P1 issue that de-registers four real, active skills. This regression needs explicit confirmation or revert before merging, and the zh-CN file should be regenerated through the pipeline rather than hand-edited.\\n\\n`extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json` and `src/plugins/bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts` — the skills removal must be explicitly confirmed or reverted. `docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md` should be regenerated via the i18n pipeline.\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003e\\u003ch3\\u003eImportant Files Changed\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n| Filename | Overview |\\n|----------|----------|\\n| docs/cli/message.md | Documentation clarified to distinguish core built-in channels from plugin-provided channels; new paragraph and bullet point added. Content is accurate and well-scoped. |\\n| docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md | Chinese translation manually updated to mirror English changes, but this file is generated and should not be directly edited; the `source_hash` frontmatter is now stale relative to the new English source. |\\n| extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json | Removes `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` from the feishu plugin manifest, but the `./skills` directory (feishu-doc, feishu-drive, feishu-perm, feishu-wiki) still exists — this appears to be an unintentional regression that de-registers all feishu skills. |\\n| src/plugins/bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts | Generated metadata file mirrors the skills removal from the feishu plugin JSON; carries the same regression risk as the source manifest change. |\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c!-- greptile_failed_comments --\\u003e\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003e\\u003ch3\\u003eComments Outside Diff (1)\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n1. `docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md`, line 1-14 ([link](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/2c203a8d73066faf76ca4fa5984e46caab0e9532/docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md#L1-L14)) \\n\\n   \\u003ca href=\\\"#\\\"\\u003e\\u003cimg alt=\\\"P2\\\" src=\\\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p2.svg?v=7\\\" align=\\\"top\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003e **Direct edit of generated i18n file; `source_hash` is now stale**\\n\\n   Per project guidelines (`CLAUDE.md`), `docs/zh-CN/**` is a generated file and should not be edited directly. The correct flow is: update English docs → run `scripts/docs-i18n` → apply targeted fixes only if instructed.\\n\\n   This file was edited by hand. As a result, the `source_hash` in the frontmatter (`35159baf...`) still reflects the old English source content before this PR's changes. Any future run of the i18n pipeline will see the Chinese file as out of sync with the English source and may overwrite these manually-applied translations.\\n\\n   Please regenerate this file via the i18n pipeline after the English changes are finalized.\\n\\n   \\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003ePrompt To Fix With AI\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n   `````markdown\\n   This is a comment left during a code review.\\n   Path: docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md\\n   Line: 1-14\\n\\n   Comment:\\n   **Direct edit of generated i18n file; `source_hash` is now stale**\\n\\n   Per project guidelines (`CLAUDE.md`), `docs/zh-CN/**` is a generated file and should not be edited directly. The correct flow is: update English docs → run `scripts/docs-i18n` → apply targeted fixes only if instructed.\\n\\n   This file was edited by hand. As a result, the `source_hash` in the frontmatter (`35159baf...`) still reflects the old English source content before this PR's changes. Any future run of the i18n pipeline will see the Chinese file as out of sync with the English source and may overwrite these manually-applied translations.\\n\\n   Please regenerate this file via the i18n pipeline after the English changes are finalized.\\n\\n   How can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n   `````\\n   \\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003c!-- /greptile_failed_comments --\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003ePrompt To Fix All With AI\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n`````markdown\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: extensions/feishu/openclaw.plugin.json\\nLine: 3\\n\\nComment:\\n**Skills entry removed while `./skills` directory still exists**\\n\\nThis PR removes `\\\"skills\\\": [\\\"./skills\\\"]` from the feishu plugin manifest, but the `./skills` directory is still fully populated with four skill definitions: `feishu-doc`, `feishu-drive`, `feishu-perm`, and `feishu-wiki`. Removing this entry means those skills will no longer be registered with the plugin, silently breaking feishu document/drive/wiki/permissions skills for any user with the feishu plugin installed.\\n\\nEvery other skills-carrying extension (`acpx`, `tavily`, `diffs`, `open-prose`) retains its `\\\"skills\\\"` entry — this removal appears accidental given the PR is scoped to documentation clarifications. The same line is also removed from `src/plugins/bundled-plugin-metadata.generated.ts` (line 839), compounding the impact.\\n\\nPlease confirm whether this removal is intentional. If not, both files need to be reverted.\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: docs/zh-CN/cli/message.md\\nLine: 1-14\\n\\nComment:\\n**Direct edit of generated i18n file; `source_hash` is now stale**\\n\\nPer project guidelines (`CLAUDE.md`), `docs/zh-CN/**` is a generated file and should not be edited directly. The correct flow is: update English docs → run `scripts/docs-i18n` → apply targeted fixes only if instructed.\\n\\nThis file was edited by hand. As a result, the `source_hash` in the frontmatter (`35159baf...`) still reflects the old English source content before this PR's changes. Any future run of the i18n pipeline will see the Chinese file as out of sync with the English source and may overwrite these manually-applied translations.\\n\\nPlease regenerate this file via the i18n pipeline after the English changes are finalized.\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n`````\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003csub\\u003eReviews (1): Last reviewed commit: [\\\"docs(cli/message): clarify plugin extens...\\\"](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/2c203a8d73066faf76ca4fa5984e46caab0e9532) | [Re-trigger Greptile](https://app.greptile.com/api/retrigger?id=26413243)\\u003c/sub\\u003e\",\"created_at\":\"2026-03-26T07:44:00Z\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/55008#issuecomment-4132388510\",\"id\":4132388510,\"issue_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/55008\",\"node_id\":\"IC_kwDOQb6kR872Tz6e\",\"performed_via_github_app\":{\"client_id\":\"Iv1.5ad00a8cea2f6701\",\"created_at\":\"2024-04-02T00:16:23Z\",\"description\":\"Greptile is an AI agent that reviews pull requests with full context of your codebase. It can catch hidden bugs, security vulnerabilities, and anti-patterns. \",\"events\":[\"issues\",\"issue_comment\",\"pull_request\",\"pull_request_review\",\"pull_request_review_comment\",\"pull_request_review_thread\"],\"external_url\":\"https://greptile.com/\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/greptile-apps\",\"id\":867647,\"name\":\"Greptile Apps\",\"node_id\":\"A_kwHOCFqEf84ADT0_\",\"owner\":{\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/140149887?v=4\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/events{/privacy}\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/gists{/gist_id}\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/greptileai\",\"id\":140149887,\"login\":\"greptileai\",\"node_id\":\"O_kgDOCFqEfw\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/orgs\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/received_events\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/repos\",\"site_admin\":false,\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/subscriptions\",\"type\":\"Organization\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\"},\"permissions\":{\"actions\":\"read\",\"checks\":\"write\",\"contents\":\"write\",\"deployments\":\"read\",\"issues\":\"write\",\"metadata\":\"read\",\"pull_requests\":\"write\",\"secret_scanning_alerts\":\"read\",\"security_events\":\"read\",\"statuses\":\"write\",\"vulnerability_alerts\":\"read\"},\"slug\":\"greptile-apps\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-11T23:07:22Z\"},\"reactions\":{\"+1\":0,\"-1\":0,\"confused\":0,\"eyes\":0,\"heart\":0,\"hooray\":0,\"laugh\":0,\"rocket\":0,\"total_count\":0,\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/comments/4132388510/reactions\"},\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-26T07:44:09Z\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/comments/4132388510\",\"user\":{\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/867647?v=4\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/greptile-apps\",\"id\":165735046,\"login\":\"greptile-apps[bot]\",\"node_id\":\"BOT_kgDOCeDqhg\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/orgs\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/received_events\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/repos\",\"site_admin\":false,\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions\",\"type\":\"Bot\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptile-apps%5Bbot%5D\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\"}}\n{\"author_association\":\"CONTRIBUTOR\",\"body\":\"\\u003ch3\\u003eGreptile Summary\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nThis PR adds documentation to `docs/channels/feishu.md` and `docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md` to explain the Feishu routing fallback chain and session key format. The intent is worthwhile — closing a diagnostics gap noted in issue #54952 — but both changed files contain the same factual inaccuracy about the role of `groupPolicy`.\\n\\n**Key issues:**\\n\\n- **Inaccurate agent-selection claim (both files):** `channels.defaults.groupPolicy` is an access-control mechanism (`open` / `allowlist` / `disabled`) that determines whether a group message is accepted, not which agent handles it. The added text in `feishu.md` says it is applied \\\"to determine which agent handles it,\\\" and the `configuration-reference.md` addition says it is used \\\"to select the handling agent.\\\" Both phrases are incorrect and could mislead operators trying to debug wrong-agent routing. The actual agent selection in the fallback path is governed by `channels.feishu.defaultAccount` (already mentioned correctly in the same sentence in `feishu.md`).\\n\\n- The session-key and fallback-chain concepts themselves are a good addition, but the `groupPolicy` role needs to be corrected in both files before this lands.\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eConfidence Score: 3/5\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\nNot safe to merge as-is — both files contain a factual inaccuracy about `groupPolicy` that would mislead operators debugging routing issues.\\n\\nThe fallback-chain and session-key additions are directionally correct and address a real documentation gap, but the same incorrect claim (that `groupPolicy` selects the handling agent) appears in both changed files. Since this is an operator-facing debugging aid, publishing inaccurate information about how agent selection works would actively harm the users this PR is trying to help. A targeted correction to the `groupPolicy` description in both files is needed before landing.\\n\\nBoth `docs/channels/feishu.md` (line 608) and `docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md` (line 86) need the `groupPolicy` agent-selection language corrected.\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003e\\u003ch3\\u003eImportant Files Changed\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n| Filename | Overview |\\n|----------|----------|\\n| docs/channels/feishu.md | Adds a fallback-chain paragraph under 'Multi-agent routing,' but incorrectly describes `channels.defaults.groupPolicy` as determining which agent handles the message rather than controlling access (open/allowlist/disabled). |\\n| docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md | Expands the `channels.defaults.groupPolicy` bullet to describe the fallback chain, but the added text inaccurately says `groupPolicy` is used 'to select the handling agent' when it is actually an access-control gate. |\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\n\\u003c!-- greptile_other_comments_section --\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cdetails\\u003e\\u003csummary\\u003ePrompt To Fix All With AI\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\n\\n`````markdown\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: docs/channels/feishu.md\\nLine: 608\\n\\nComment:\\n**`groupPolicy` controls access, not agent selection**\\n\\nThe sentence states the gateway \\\"applies the **default group policy** (`channels.defaults.groupPolicy`) to determine which agent handles it,\\\" but this is inaccurate. `groupPolicy` is an access-control value (`open` / `allowlist` / `disabled`) that decides whether a group message is *accepted at all* — not which agent processes it. The handling agent in the fallback case is determined by the default account (already mentioned in the same sentence), not by `groupPolicy`.\\n\\nConsider rewording to separate these two concerns:\\n\\n```suggestion\\nWhen no explicit binding matches an incoming message, the gateway falls back to the **default account** (configured via `channels.feishu.defaultAccount` or the first configured account) to select the handling agent. The **default group policy** (`channels.defaults.groupPolicy`) then determines whether the message is accepted (`open`/`allowlist`/`disabled`). The resulting canonical session key (e.g., `feishu:g-{chatId}` for groups) reflects the final routing destination but does not encode which rule — binding or fallback — was responsible.\\n```\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThis is a comment left during a code review.\\nPath: docs/gateway/configuration-reference.md\\nLine: 86\\n\\nComment:\\n**`groupPolicy` selects access, not the handling agent**\\n\\nThe added sentence \\\"the gateway applies this default policy to **select the handling agent**\\\" misrepresents what `groupPolicy` does. As confirmed by the source implementation in `src/security/dm-policy-shared.ts` and `src/security/audit-channel.ts`, `groupPolicy` is an access-control gate (`open` / `allowlist` / `disabled`) — it determines whether a group message is allowed through, not which agent it is dispatched to. Agent selection in the fallback case is governed by `defaultAccount`, not `groupPolicy`.\\n\\n```suggestion\\n- `channels.defaults.groupPolicy`: fallback group policy when a provider-level `groupPolicy` is unset. Accepted values are `open`, `allowlist`, and `disabled`; this policy controls whether inbound group messages are accepted when no explicit binding matches, not which agent handles them. The session key's canonical form reflects the final destination but does not indicate whether routing matched a binding or the fallback chain.\\n```\\n\\nHow can I resolve this? If you propose a fix, please make it concise.\\n`````\\n\\n\\u003c/details\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003csub\\u003eReviews (1): Last reviewed commit: [\\\"docs: clarify Feishu routing fallback ch...\\\"](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/c6f8f3f44b4c67a83b8e378ef75363870d95e027) | [Re-trigger Greptile](https://app.greptile.com/api/retrigger?id=26413689)\\u003c/sub\\u003e\",\"created_at\":\"2026-03-26T07:50:24Z\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/55013#issuecomment-4132433687\",\"id\":4132433687,\"issue_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/openclaw/openclaw/issues/55013\",\"node_id\":\"IC_kwDOQb6kR872T-8X\",\"performed_via_github_app\":{\"client_id\":\"Iv1.5ad00a8cea2f6701\",\"created_at\":\"2024-04-02T00:16:23Z\",\"description\":\"Greptile is an AI agent that reviews pull requests with full context of your codebase. It can catch hidden bugs, security vulnerabilities, and anti-patterns. \",\"events\":[\"issues\",\"issue_comment\",\"pull_request\",\"pull_request_review\",\"pull_request_review_comment\",\"pull_request_review_thread\"],\"external_url\":\"https://greptile.com/\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/greptile-apps\",\"id\":867647,\"name\":\"Greptile Apps\",\"node_id\":\"A_kwHOCFqEf84ADT0_\",\"owner\":{\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/140149887?v=4\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/events{/privacy}\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/gists{/gist_id}\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/greptileai\",\"id\":140149887,\"login\":\"greptileai\",\"node_id\":\"O_kgDOCFqEfw\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/orgs\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/greptileai/received_events\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/